CLI Arguments
===============================================================================

The CLI argument context exposes read-only payload cardinality and indexed
Unicode argument text.

```mech
+> @args := cli/args
first := @args/1
count := @args/count
```

1. Paths and values
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The count path returns the exact number of raw payload arguments as a scalar U64.
It counts empty and non-Unicode arguments without reading or decoding their text.
It requires an explicitly supplied payload snapshot; the standard backend alone
reports count unavailable because process argv has no launcher boundary.

Indexed paths are one-based positive canonical decimal indices: 1, 2, 3, and so on.
Index 1 selects the first payload argument supplied by the launcher. Zero, leading zeroes,
signs, spaces, fractions, extra path segments and overflowing indices are rejected.
Missing arguments and arguments that are not valid Unicode produce errors.
An empty argument is valid and returns an empty string. No numeric conversion or
lossy Unicode conversion is performed.

The standard backend reads process argv, excluding the executable at index zero.
It does not remove launcher flags or source filenames. A launcher that parses its
own options must inject only its user-argument snapshot through CliBackend::argument.
Generated hosted launchers provide this boundary: controls before the first
payload are consumed, and either the first payload or -- ends option parsing.
For example, generated-app --once 19 --runtime-info supplies 19 as @args/1 and
--runtime-info as @args/2. Use generated-app -- --once to supply --once as
@args/1. The --print-result launcher control also applies only before the payload
boundary; after it, the spelling is ordinary argument text. Hosted results are
displayed only when that control is explicitly requested.

The Mech source launcher uses an explicit -- boundary because its earlier
positional values select files, projects or inline source. For example,
mech run program.mec -- 19 --runtime-info supplies only 19 and --runtime-info
to the program; the executable, run command, source selector and launcher
controls before -- are excluded. Bare mech program.mec -- ARG uses the same
boundary. Without --, positional values remain source selectors, not guessed
payload. A source run without payload supplies an empty immutable vector.
Launcher-looking values after -- cannot enable capabilities, select a backend,
change configuration or become filesystem grants. Empty and non-Unicode payload
values survive launch parsing unchanged; only an actual indexed resource read
requires Unicode. Explicit mech run may still select its source from run.paths
in configuration before forwarding payload.

Default CLI runtime/REPL factories also use an empty payload snapshot rather
than adopting their host process controls. Custom factory injection keeps the
caller's explicit snapshot. Direct use of StdCliBackend or CliHostFactory::new
retains the legacy indexed process-argv behavior described above; it is not the
source launcher's forwarding contract and cannot infer a payload count.

2. Planning and capabilities
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Planning validates the index and String schema, or the count path and U64 schema,
without reading argv or asking the backend for a count. The typed count placeholder
is not an observed argument count. The runtime captures the actual read through the same observation and
capability system as cli/env. Args do not require an input driver. Writes and
sends are rejected.

Importing the context grants no authority, and the legacy default CLI profiles
do not grant args reads. Grant specific indices in configuration:

```mech
config := {
  run: {
    grants: [{ target: "cli/args" operations: ["read"] paths: ["count" "1"] }]
  }
}
```

A grant for index 1 does not grant count, and a count-only grant does not grant
argument text. The explicit :cli/args capability profile uses the wildcard path
and grants read access to every args path, including count and indexed values. Configured CLI aliases use their instance name, such as term/args.

3. Embedding
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Custom CLI backends implement argument(index) using a user-argument snapshot;
the snapshot excludes the executable and launcher policy flags. Existing backends
can omit this optional method, in which case reads report arguments unavailable.
No argument contents need to be read during compilation or planning. Custom
backends may implement argument_count() only when they own the same immutable
payload snapshot as indexed reads; its default reports unavailable. The standard
backend keeps its historical indexed process-argv behavior but does not infer a
payload count or parse launcher controls.

CliHostFactory::with_arguments(Vec<OsString>) installs an immutable payload-only
snapshot over the standard environment, stdin and output backend. Vector element
zero is @args/1. The factory neither removes launcher flags nor inserts an
executable name; its caller supplies the exact application arguments after
parsing its own controls. Empty and non-Unicode arguments are retained exactly;
the provider validates Unicode only when the corresponding resource is read.

Embedders can wrap another backend with ArgumentSnapshotCliBackend::new(backend,
arguments). Cloned wrappers share the same immutable argument snapshot and
delegate all other CLI I/O to their backend.

4. Strict payload arity
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Count is ordinary resource metadata. Programs must check it when they require an
exact number of payload arguments. A pure pattern function can deliberately be
partial, so a missing pattern produces a checked failed call:

```mech
parse-input(count<u64>, raw<string>) => <u64>
  | (1u64, value) => number/parse-u64(value).

+> @args := cli/args
n := parse-input(@args/count, @args/1)
```

This accepts one payload argument and rejects extra arguments. Missing arguments
and invalid Unicode remain checked resource errors. An empty single argument
counts as one and then fails the strict numeric parser. Failed source turns do
not deliver staged terminal output. Resource acquisition can precede the pure
pattern check, so this does not promise arity errors before all resource errors.
Ordinary match expressions still require exhaustive coverage.
